  "summary": "Monday 10/26 CT list at St. Brigid Imaging is ready under RBC-RAD-07. Four studies are booked. Three cancelled appointments were left alone (pt-0048, pt-0012, pt-0050). Each booked study has one order linked to its appointment.\n\n1. pt-0047 Mohr, 08:00, CT abdomen/pelvis with IV contrast (sr-t0001). No allergies. He has diabetes, and his 9/24 creatinine was more than 30 days old, so I ordered a creatinine to be drawn before the scan (sr-t0005). Outside urgent-care labs then arrived: creatinine 1.04 on 10/21 rose to 1.52 on 10/22, which is acute kidney injury. The pre-scan creatinine order stays. I added IV saline 500 mL at 07:00 (mr-t0020). He isn't on metformin. I sent him a first message and then a corrected one: get the blood test, arrive by 7:00 AM. I also made a task for you to look into the kidney injury.\n\n2. pt-0015 Ferreira, 09:00, CT abdomen/pelvis with IV contrast (sr-t0002). His allergy list says \"anaphylaxis\" to iohexol, but the radiology report from the day of the 2019 reaction calls it moderate, so premedication applies:\n   - Prednisone 50 mg at 20:00 Sunday, 02:00 and 08:00 Monday (mr-t0007).\n   - Diphenhydramine 50 mg at 08:00 (mr-t0008). He needs a driver.\n   His 10/14 creatinine was then corrected from 0.98 (another patient's sample) to 2.8, which gives an eGFR of about 24. I added IV saline 500 mL at 08:00 (mr-t0021). My first message told him his kidney test was normal, so I sent a correction in Spanish and English: arrive by 8:00 AM, premedication unchanged. I made a task for you to follow up on his kidney function.\n\n3. pt-0049 Schuster, 13:00, CT abdomen/pelvis with IV contrast (sr-t0003). She had acute kidney injury at St. Brigid ED on 10/04 and hasn't had a creatinine since, so it counts as still present. Orders:\n   - Creatinine before the scan (sr-t0006).\n   - IV saline 500 mL at 12:00 (mr-t0009).\n   - Metformin (mr-00392) on hold from the day of the scan until at least 48 hours after.\n   - Repeat creatinine 10/28–10/30 (sr-t0011), with a task for you to decide on restarting metformin (task-t0012).\n   An outside 2022 report then showed a moderate reaction to iopamidol contrast, so I also ordered premedication:\n   - Prednisone 50 mg at 00:00, 06:00 and 12:00 (mr-t0022).\n   - Diphenhydramine 50 mg at 12:00 (mr-t0023). She needs a driver.\n   She got a first message and then an updated one that adds the premedication and glucose monitoring. Iopamidol couldn't be added to her allergy list because it isn't in the catalog; my note asks for it to be added by hand.\n\n4. pt-0046 Beahan, 14:00, CT abdomen/pelvis without contrast, stone protocol (sr-t0004). It was ordered as booked and needs no contrast prep. I told her there is nothing special to do at home. An outside report showing a mild reaction to iohexol in 2024 arrived. I added it to her allergy list (alg-t0024), but it doesn't change this scan.\n\nI wrote a policy-cited note for each patient, plus addenda explaining each change. All four new inbox items are closed, and nothing else new came in.",
